React Router extension of React, yes. This allow client-side rendering. Listen. Listen. Normal website change page with server request. Client-side rendering good because it change URL and update web page without ask server for new document or element. Rocky show example of React Router program.

```javascript
import React from "react";
import { createRoot } from "react-dom/client";
import {createBrowserRouter, RouterProvider, Route, Link} from "react-router-dom";

const router = createBrowserRouter([
  {
    path: "/",
    element: (
      <div>
        <h1>Hello Agent</h1>
        <Link to="about">About</Link>
      </div>
    ),
  },
  {
    path: "about",
    element: <div>This is the About Page</div>,
  },
]);

createRoot(document.getElementById("root")).render(
  <RouterProvider router={router} />
);
```

This React Router program have routes. Default page show heading with "Hello Agent" and link to other route call "about." This lead to page with just div that say "This is the About Page." Good, good, good.

